  "prediction": "**Rescue Mission: University Professor Helps Save Student's Family from ISIS**\n\nIn a remarkable story of courage and determination, Charlotta Turner, a professor of Analytical Chemistry at Lund University in Sweden, played a crucial role in rescuing her student Firas Jumaah's family from the clutches of ISIS in Iraq in 2014.\n\nFiras Jumaah, a Yazidi, had returned to his native Iraq to visit his family, but soon found himself in a desperate situation as ISIS began to advance on the city. His family, including his wife and two small children, were in grave danger, and Jumaah sent a text message to Turner, asking her to remove him from the doctoral program if he wasn't back in Sweden within a week.\n\nTurner, who had been Jumaah's supervisor, was shocked by the message and quickly realized the gravity of the situation. She contacted the university's security chief, Per Gustafson, who arranged for a rescue operation to be carried out by a security company.\n\nOver a few days, Gustafson and the security team worked tirelessly to plan and execute the rescue, which involved hiring heavily-armed mercenaries to extract Jumaah and his family from the war-torn area. The team successfully rescued the family, who were hiding in a bleach factory, and transported them to Erbil Airport, where they were flown to safety.\n\nThe rescue operation was a unique and unprecedented event, with no other university involved in a similar mission. Jumaah and his family were eventually reunited in Sweden, where Jumaah completed his PhD and now works as a pharmaceuticals professional in Malmö.\n\nThe family has almost finished paying back the university for the rescue operation, which was a testament to the bravery and selflessness of Turner and Gustafson. The story serves as a reminder of the importance of human connection and the power of individuals to make a difference in the face of adversity.\n\nTurner's actions were motivated by her desire to help her student and his family, and she has said that it was a \"unique and spontaneous\" decision to get involved in the rescue operation. The story has been widely reported and has highlighted the bravery and compassion of Turner and the university's security team.\n\nIn an interview with NBC News, Turner said, \"It was very spontaneous. For me, it was obvious that I should help and bring them home.\" The rescue operation was a remarkable example of the university's commitment to its students and its willingness to go above and beyond to ensure their safety and well-being",
